page :life_in_the_system, title: "Life in the System", wiki: "Life in the System" do
  summary "An overview of ordinary life across the inhabited Kaleidos system."
  status :draft
  category :lore

  prose <<~PROSE
    A household on Korvath can buy grain grown along its own river, while a family in Kaleidos orbit eats produce from several habs. Relatives cross between worlds for weddings, students take rooms near their teachers, and travelers learn which local hours a host means by morning. The well-traveled routes join communities whose ordinary days remain different.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, heading: "The Basics"
    Kaleidos and #{ref :korvath, "Korvath"} grow much of the system's food, alongside orbital greenhouses whose crops follow light and temperature set by their keepers. A meal in a ring hab can combine surface grain, greenhouse protein, and fruit from the next fragment. Water passes through filters and returns to use. Salvaged metal joins newly mined stock in kitchens, workshops, and hulls.

    Small #{encyclopedia_ref :ringglass, "ringglass"} components bring ambient #{encyclopedia_ref :resonance, "resonance"} into lanterns, stoves, heaters, compasses, and household communication sets. Replacement inserts are sold beside the devices they fit. A failing kitchen lamp can be repaired at the table while dinner continues under the one by the door. Workshops cut and tune inexpensive stock for those ordinary uses; a household seldom needs the intact ring-era glass collectors seek.

    Builders mix ringglass into structural materials to reinforce walls, foundations, and pressure seals. Supply and ground conditions change how much they use. On Korvath, imported cuts concentrate the weak local field around cranes and hospitals while a village mill may still turn with water. Ceramic and loose aggregate separate large arrays from native stone that would carry their pulses into neighboring structures.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, heading: "Paying for Things"
    Standard credits pay a dock fee at #{ref :glasswake, "Glasswake"}, a berth in a ring hab, or a grain purchase in Sithari. Barter runs beside them: a repair for meals, spare cargo space for help at the destination, an instrument lent through a festival in return for a place to sleep. A traveler arranging a long stay asks what the host wants as well as what the room costs. Some habs admit guests through household invitations or shared work and have little use for the fare that brought them there.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, heading: "Mended Things"
    Along the ring routes, repaired equipment is worn openly and good mending carries status. Each trade mends in its own recognizable way — a keel crew's graft-joins do not look like a Tuner's seam-work. Components salvaged from the ring era sit beside modern manufacture in ordinary equipment. Other communities place more value on new work, inherited possessions or materials grown close to home.

    Working technology carries a hum just below perception; a room full of running devices feels occupied even in silence. Craft that survives from before the #{ref :the_glassfall, "Glassfall"} is different in the hand — too smooth and too fractal at the same time, finished at every scale a person can examine and at several they cannot.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, heading: "Whose Writ Runs"
    #{ref :sithari, "Sithari"} has strong influence along the core routes. The #{ref :tempered_accord, "Tempered Accord"} mediates among participating settlements, whose own institutions carry out the resulting agreements. Other communities rely on different alliances or on arrangements with their nearest neighbors.

    A traveler can leave a port governed by a league of towns and enter #{ref :fermata_station, "Fermata Station"}, where a public performance can settle a contested use of shared rooms. Korvathi river towns move gates and workshops together as channels change. At #{ref :outside, "Outside"}, households receive guests in their own rooms while the Displacement Council holds the nearby cordon. A host, a harbor, and a government can each make different decisions about the same arrival.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, heading: "Getting Around"
    #{encyclopedia_ref :flitter, "Flitters"} carry local passengers and light cargo. Road haulers, barges, and larger #{encyclopedia_ref :kite, "kites"} serve loads whose weight or route calls for another vessel. Booking passage between major settlements is routine, and most people in the inner system have visited at least one other world. Municipal fleets, cooperatives, family carriers, and companies such as #{ref :vantara, "Vantara"} carry different parts of a journey.

    Fast ships reach an adjacent planet in about a day and the outer system in about a week. That makes a visit to relatives possible within a short leave, provided a vessel has room at a price the traveler can pay. A berth aboard a faction's supply ship may be the only passage to a distant settlement for some time. #{ref :ithara, "Ithara"} receives fairly frequent faction traffic, but a household letter still needs someone willing to carry it; the general post does not reach Lithren.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, heading: "What People Do"
    Sports leagues hold seasonal tournaments that draw spectators from several worlds. Players carry regional strategy games into ship messes and teach them while waiting for a transfer. Competitive Tuning brings practitioners to the same rooms as people who came to hear or feel the finished work. The #{ref :the_fermata_open, "Fermata Open"} fills borrowed bedrooms with visiting musicians and gives listeners a week of performances extending through the hab.

    #{encyclopedia_ref :velith, "Velith"} verses travel from Istrava into workrooms and family meals. At Outside, younger diners turn a visitor's compliment into a reply about someone seated beside them. Households with Oskaran ties exchange bread, pictures, and news of the animals their friends gave them. A passenger arriving with a remembered refrain can be invited to supper before anyone knows their occupation.

    Leisure habs contain gardens, casinos, bathing rooms, and attractions built around their unusual spaces. In rooms of changing gravity, visitors leap between terraces and land on what looked like a wall from the launch platform. Others climb a surface ridge, watch the broken ring from a quiet observation room, or take a cruise along the Shear's charted edge. Time away and passage fare determine who can make those journeys. Families also spend holidays in nearby rooms, hosting relatives who have saved enough to come to them.

    Surface seasons still govern harvests, weather, and outdoor gatherings. Relatives on different worlds keep several such calendars in one household: a home harvest, a child's school recess, the festival at the place a partner left. They exchange recordings when those dates cannot be made into a shared visit.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, heading: "The Everyday Improbable"
    Millions of habs contain more kinds of living space than any visitor can encounter. A conventional corridor may lead to a household whose customs take patient learning. Elsewhere, an interior preserves a deliberately engineered climate, or years of cultivation have turned walls and decks into living ground.

    In habs with fluid space, familiar movements can depend on conditions a newcomer has yet to recognize. At Fermata, a sustained note can reach a weak panel through the frame; residents leave the chalked compartment quiet while continuing to play next door. At a cordon town, a changed pulse from the wall interrupts conversation because its residents know what that particular return precedes.

    Local builders accommodate repeated effects in walls, foundations, and room use. Visitors learn from the people who sleep and work there: which door sticks in a shift, where a handhold remains within reach, when the familiar hum calls for attention. The knowledge travels with former residents, while its usefulness depends on what the next place actually does.
  PROSE
end
